      "writtenOn": "2014-04-10T13:09:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since we are checking the same flow in all four methods, wouldn\u0027t it make sense to combine both verify_password_length and trunc_password? \n\nThat way the hash_passwords methods just have to call def verify_and_trunc_password() and they get back what ever is appropriate. Then we only have to check CONF.strict_password_length in one place too. This might make it easier to maintain in the future and should prevent regression. \n\n    def hash_password(password):\n        \"\"\"Hash a password. Hard.\"\"\"\n        password_utf8 \u003d verify_and_trunc_password(password).encode(\u0027utf-8\u0027)\n        return passlib.hash.sha512_crypt.encrypt(password_utf8, rounds\u003dCONF.crypt_strength)\n\n    def verify_and_trunc_password(password):\n        max_length \u003d CONF.identity.max_password_length\n        try:\n            if len(password) \u003e max_length and CONF.strict_password_length:\n                #raise because we have strict password checking enabled\n                raise exception.ValidationError\n            elif len(password) \u003e max_length and not CONF.strict_password_length:\n                #go ahead and truncate since we don\u0027t have strict password checking enabled.\n                LOG.warning()\n                return password[:max_length]\n            else:\n                #password length checks out\n                return password\n        except TypeError:\n            ....\n\nThoughts?",
